<body>
<div class="wrap">

<header class="mark">
  <div class="wordmark">Limen</div>
  <p class="etym">līmen — the threshold. Everything below it never reaches the model.</p>
  <p class="claim">A decision layer between your coding agent and its model API. It answers,
    before the call is made, what the request does not need to carry:
    <b>{{HEAD_PCT}}%</b> fewer input tokens on measured traffic, task pass-rate held, running
    on your own machine at <b>$0</b>.</p>
  <nav class="top">
    <a href="#install">install</a>
    <a href="#numbers">the numbers</a>
    <a href="#use">how to use it</a>
    <a href="#cost">what it costs</a>
    <a href="#gates">what is not measured</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/aashish254/limen">source</a>
  </nav>
</header>

<section id="hero" aria-label="one real request, before and after">
  <div class="sechead"><span class="meta">the product</span><h2>one request, and what goes away</h2></div>
  <p>One request from the demo corpus, replayed twice: once with every slot observing,
  once with two of them enforcing. Both numbers below are the input the provider was
  billed for, and the printout is the enforcing run's — the command the tool itself
  tells you to run. Struck columns are what Limen removed; the reason beside each cut is
  <em>not</em> struck, because the reason is the part you audit.</p>
  {{HERO}}
  <p class="stamp">rendered verbatim from <b>{{CMD_HERO}}</b> at commit <b>{{SHA}}</b>.
  <code>delivered</code> means the bytes never reached the model; <code>potential</code>
  means the slot named a cut it was not switched on to make. Nothing left this machine:
  the upstream is the in-repo mock.</p>
</section>

<section id="what">
  <div class="sechead"><span class="meta">what it is</span><h2>four decisions, made locally, in the time of one regex</h2></div>
  <p>Limen is a proxy you point your agent at. It speaks the OpenAI, Anthropic and Gemini
  request shapes, records every decision it makes, and answers four questions before your
  prompt is sent: which tool specs this task cannot use, which old turns have gone stale,
  which file notes the graph says are irrelevant, and how much reasoning this step deserves.
  It is a <em>System One</em> layer — fast, cheap, fallible — sitting under the model rather
  than replacing it.</p>
  <p>It ships with no third-party runtime dependencies, needs no account, and starts in
  <span class="state-warn">observation</span>: it records what it would have dropped and
  changes nothing until you name the slots you trust.</p>
  <table class="slots">
    <caption>The four slots. None of them is on until you name it in
      <code>SUBPROTO_APPLY</code>, and each one is reversible on its own. The token column is
      the offline projection <code>bench/ab.py --mock</code> prints — what the slot could take
      from recorded prompt shapes, not yet what it took.</caption>
    <tr><th>slot</th><th>what it decides</th><th class="n">tokens it would cut</th><th class="n">% of input</th></tr>
    {{SLOT_TABLE}}
  </table>
</section>

<section id="install">
  <div class="sechead"><span class="meta">install</span><h2>three routes, no account at any of them</h2></div>
  <div class="routes">
    <div class="route">
      <h3>from the source you just cloned</h3>
      <pre>git clone https://github.com/aashish254/limen.git
cd limen
./install.sh                <span class="c"># puts `subproto` on PATH, no package index</span></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="route">
      <h3>into a virtualenv you already use</h3>
      <pre>python -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; source .venv/bin/activate
pip install .               <span class="c"># Python 3.9 or newer, zero dependencies</span></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="route">
      <h3>without installing anything</h3>
      <pre>python3 -m subproto demo --slots   <span class="c"># replays 17 agent turns through the proxy</span></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>The package and the command are still named <code>subproto</code>; <b>Limen</b> is the
  project. Point your agent at it and check the machine before you believe anything else:</p>
  <pre>subproto doctor            <span class="c"># 6 measured checks: interpreter, data dir, port, keys, backends</span>
subproto demo --slots      <span class="c"># 17 synthetic agent requests through the proxy, against the local mock</span>
subproto report            <span class="c"># what those requests would have cost, and what the slots would have cut</span></pre>
  <p class="stamp"><b>{{TESTS}}</b> tests and <b>{{MUTANTS}}</b> mutants are the gate:
  <code>bash run_tests.sh</code> runs them, and CI runs that on Python {{PYVERSIONS}} on Linux
  and macOS.</p>
</section>

<section id="numbers">
  <div class="sechead"><span class="meta">the numbers</span><h2>the same traffic, with the system off and on</h2></div>
  <p>Every figure below comes out of a run on this machine at $0 against the in-repo mock.
  The corpus is {{NTASKS}} coding-agent tasks replayed {{ITERS}} times through three arms:
  <em>observe</em> (nothing is changed), <em>enforce</em> (each slot spends its own budget),
  and <em>compiled</em> (one joint budget over the whole request). Where a number is a
  projection or has not been run, the caption says which.</p>
  {{FIGURES}}
  <table class="pool">
    <caption>The pooled result, in text, so the figures are not the only record. Pooled
      over every task and repeat; the change column is <em>observe → compiled</em> unless
      the row says otherwise.</caption>
    <tr><th>metric</th><th class="n">without</th><th class="n">per-slot</th><th class="n">compiled</th><th class="n">change</th></tr>
    {{POOL_TABLE}}
  </table>
  <p class="stamp">all panels generated by <b>{{CMD}}</b> at commit <b>{{SHA}}</b> on
  <b>{{HOST}}</b>, python <b>{{PYTHON}}</b>, load average <b>{{LOAD}}</b> at start.
  Re-run that command and the page rebuilds from what it measures.</p>
</section>

<section id="use">
  <div class="sechead"><span class="meta">how to use it</span><h2>the ladder, and where each rung earns its keep</h2></div>
  <div class="ladder">
    <div><h3>1 — observe</h3><p>Set the base URL and record. Nothing is rewritten, and the
      first page you read is <code>subproto report</code>: what your own traffic carries that
      it does not need.</p></div>
    <div><h3>2 — enforce what you trust</h3><p>Name the slots, one at a time:
      <code>SUBPROTO_APPLY=tool_gate</code>. Each one is reversible, and the pass-rate gate
      is checked per arm, not pooled.</p></div>
    <div><h3>3 — compile</h3><p><code>SUBPROTO_COMPILE=on</code> spends a single budget over
      messages, tool specs and file notes together. Cheaper and more precise — and slower per
      decision, which is stated below rather than hidden.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>The cached prefix is never rewritten. That is the whole reason a token-saving proxy can
  be worth anything: busting a provider's prompt cache costs more than the tokens it saves, so
  Limen only ever edits the part of the request the cache does not hold, and there is an
  invariant test that asserts byte equality on it.</p>
</section>

<section id="cost">
  <div class="sechead"><span class="meta">the honest part</span><h2>what it costs when it is wrong</h2></div>
  <p>{{COST_PROSE}}</p>
  {{FIG_REGRET}}
</section>

<section id="gates">
  <div class="sechead"><span class="meta">not measured</span><h2>the claims this page does not make</h2></div>
  <table>
    <caption>Every one of these is a human action or a spend decision, and each is written into
      <code>TODO.md</code> as gated rather than checked off.</caption>
    <tr><th>claim</th><th>what it would take</th></tr>
    {{GATE_TABLE}}
  </table>
  <p>Against other systems: the published comparison for a decision layer of this kind is
  TypeSafe AI's Jev, a hosted classifier at $0.042 per million tokens. Limen is local, Apache-2.0
  and bills nothing. <b>We have never run Jev</b>, so this page carries no speed or accuracy
  multiplier against it — only the axis we can stand on: what our own traffic costs with the
  layer on and off.</p>
</section>

